2026-09-02T22:54Z claude note THIRD ADAPTER, AND THE FIRST THAT IS NOT A PROCESS THE HARNESS OWNS. ZotSeek 1.21.2, commit f442f82, artifact zotseek-1.21.2.xpi sha256 7daaf964 (103 284 735 bytes) from update.json's update_link, digest verified at construction rather than trusted. It loads on Zotero 10.0.1 on padme and it is inspectable; no line of it is reproduced here, everything is cited path:line or measured. Run: 1 pass, 1 fail, 3 not-offered, 0 not-run (bench/results/0584-zotseek/acceptance.json). FIVE VERBS DECLARED ABSENT, each for a different reason, and two of those reasons are kinds of absence neither other adapter has. (1) uninstall: the surface EXISTS and does real work -- bootstrap.js:90-141 deletes the sidecar database with its journal, wal and shm siblings and deleteBranch()es the entire preference branch, for reason 6 (ADDON_UNINSTALL) only -- and nothing a machine can do fires it. Measured WITH A CONTROL ARM: deleting the sideloaded XPI while the host is stopped and restarting does not fire the hook, the plugin's own bootstrap lines still appear in the restarted session's debug output, the sidecar and all sixteen preference keys survive, and the string 'uninstall' does not occur once in 1 691 lines of debug output; the control arm, an identical restart with the XPI left in place, is indistinguishable. That is a third kind of absence next to zoteus's 'no such surface' and zotero-mcp's 'the documented removal is uninstalling the package'. (2) pause/resume: the target HAS unattended machinery (src/core/auto-index-manager.ts:22-45) and HAS a real pause control, a button in its progress window (src/utils/stable-progress.ts:602-615, read by src/index.ts:1433 waitIfPaused()); the machinery is off by default and the control is GUI-only, so neither is reachable. Architecturally opposite to zotero-mcp's 'there is no such work at all'. (3) query and (4) status follow the lane ruling: the REST/MCP endpoints are real and gated on zotseek.mcpServer.enabled, shipped false and commented 'Opt-in' (src/server/server-manager.ts:23,30-40,71-75); measured 404 on the running default instance; enabling it is the non-default option the contract forbids and would change R10's subject by opening a socket the default does not open. status was MEASURED rather than assumed per the lane instruction: the sidecar is readable with no opt-in and carries NO status -- one metadata row, schema_version, with the model in effect living in a host preference instead (src/core/model-registry.ts:103-115) -- so constructing one would be a workaround. Ticket 0597's reason field is what keeps these five distinct; without it they are one cell, and this target is where it earns its place three times over.
2026-09-02T22:55Z claude note SEVEN PLACES THE INTERFACE COULD NOT EXPRESS THIS TARGET, all recorded in the adapter's module docstring where the next adapter's author reads them. (1) derived_state_roots presumes the target owns its state: this target's entire default-configuration footprint is two files and one empty directory (data/zotseek.sqlite, profile/zotseek-models/, plus sixteen preference keys) and every directory containing them is the host's. (2) A host-only baseline CANNOT BE SUBTRACTED AT PATH GRANULARITY, which is worse than it sounds: measured two launches of the same application, one with the plugin and one without, 311 files existed only in the host-only arm and 317 only in the with-plugin arm, and all of that difference except four paths is the host's own churn -- it opens its first-run start page in the desktop browser and stages its word-processor integration, both inheriting the sandbox HOME, both naming their directories with a fresh random string per run. So home/ and profile/ are exempted directory-wide with that argument, and the data directory entry by entry from the host-only arm, which keeps the sweep able to fail in the one directory the target does write to. (3) A preference key is derived state no path can name: sixteen zotseek.* keys go into the host's profile/prefs.js and none appear in the host-only arm; declaring the file claims the host's whole preference store, omitting it hides state the target creates, so it is listed under not_derived_state as an admission with its argument. (4) not_derived_state is documented for user data and external configuration and here has to carry the HOST APPLICATION, which is neither. (5) check_no_egress requires the subject's return code to be zero, so a target whose process does not exit on its own can never reach pass; this adapter escapes only because the drive subprocess owns the host and stops it. (6) The lifecycle assumes starting the target starts the target -- here it starts a desktop application which then loads the plugin, so readiness has to be a file the PLUGIN writes (zotseek.sqlite) or a host that came up with a plugin that did not load reads as green. (7) query_transport presumes a wire and this target has none: verified at the lane lead's request, it reads the library through the host's in-process data layer (Zotero.Items.getAsync, Zotero.Search, Zotero.Libraries, Zotero.Fulltext.getPages, attachment.attachmentText -- src/utils/zotero-api.ts:203-241,376-421,474; src/core/hybrid-search.ts:344,385-386) and ATTACHes its sidecar to the host's LIVE SQLite connection (src/core/vector-store-sqlite.ts:267-293), never over the local HTTP API; the only occurrence of that port in its source is prose telling a user how to point an MCP client at it (src/server/mcp-endpoint.ts:10). This falsifies the harness's assumption that a target reads Zotero over a wire. One place the interface came out AHEAD: nothing else could have separated the five absences above.
2026-09-02T22:56Z claude note R10 IS RED AND THE RED IS THE HOST'S, with a matched pair to price it. Subject arm: 0 off-machine connection attempts, 40 name lookups, all to the stub resolver at 127.0.0.53:53, return code 0; both controls tripped both detectors. A matched pair differing in NOTHING but the presence of the XPI -- same launcher, same profile shape, the same five harness preferences, same mechanism, same tracer, and a FIXED 120 s dwell in both arms because a readiness-driven dwell is not the same length in the two -- reads 0/56 without the plugin and 0/60 with it, EACH REPRODUCED TO THE DIGIT across two independent replicates, against 47/5 with the route intact. So the target adds four name lookups and no connection attempt whatever, and the clause reds on the host's fifty-six. The arena state was controlled for and is stated: a VIRGIN profile and data directory created fresh inside the arena on every construction, never seeded, never cleared; the lane lead's own host-only baseline moves 426 -> 60 on arena state alone, which is why saying which state was run matters. AND THE TRACE CANNOT ATTRIBUTE THE FOUR: every lookup in both arms issues from a single pid, the host's main process, which is where this target's JavaScript runs. A process tracer has no finer resolution than a process and an in-process plugin has no process of its own, so on this architecture class R10's instrument resolves to the host and stops. What makes those four lookups is NOT ESTABLISHED and this adapter does not guess. The verdict recorded is the measured fail rather than a manufactured not-run: the adapter has no channel to say 'undecidable on my architecture' -- unsupported maps only to not-offered and the four verbs the clause drives are not absent -- so the honest act is to record the red and put both control arms in the declaration where a reader meets them. Nothing is subtracted in code; that would be the layer scoring a result.
2026-09-02T22:57Z claude note TWO DEFECTS THE OFFLINE TESTS COULD NOT HAVE FOUND, both from the first real run. The residue sweep came back red with exactly one file in 931 created: host.log, written by this adapter to capture the host's output. The arena is documented as harness-owned but the sweep counts every file in it as the target's, so an adapter cannot instrument inside its own arena without declaring the instrument; declared as one file with the argument, and piping instead was rejected because this host is a desktop application whose stdout is the only diagnostic when it fails to come up. And stopping the host by signalling the direct child left a reparented process alive -- the launcher is a shell script and the application starts the desktop browser and the office suite's registration helper -- which hangs the egress tracer, since strace -f waits for descendants; observed on a control arm whose traced run never returned. The stop now signals the process group. THIRTEEN ARMS DRIVEN RED FROM A FIXTURE against the committed tree before any green was accepted: digest check removed; data/ exempted wholesale; host-data exemptions dropped; readiness returning unconditionally; the preference prefix filter dropped; a target preference smuggled into harness setup; status() returning instead of raising; two absent verbs sharing one reason; the port hardcoded away from the constructor; install() claiming state that does not exist; a target name planted in the layer (the neutrality guard picks this adapter up from its own NAMES with no edit); the host.log exemption removed; and that exemption widened to the arena. Each fired on its intended guard and only there. THE TRAP THAT WOULD HAVE MADE THE DECLARATION WRONG: the preference file shipped inside the XPI is NOT the effective default configuration -- it declares maxTokens 800 and indexingMode 'abstract' where a first run writes 2000 and 'full', plus four keys the shipped file does not contain at all -- so the effective set is read back from the profile and reported as measured. NOTE FOR THE LADDER, from the lead's second question: this target runs against a fresh, empty, harness-owned Zotero profile and data directory, so an assertion declined elsewhere for touching the author's own library is decidable here; what it could drive is index durability rather than library writes, since the target appears read-only against the library (I did not read PR #226 and did not implement against it).
--- body ---
## Context

The ratified target-neutral harness assigns its in-process-plugin architecture
seat to `introfini/ZotSeek` while that project remains runnable and inspectable.
Ticket 0578 builds the layer and first adapter. ZotSeek declares MIT only in
`package.json` and has no LICENSE file, so this work may run and inspect it but
must not copy its code into this repository.

## Actions

1. Pin a runnable ZotSeek revision and record the eligibility check.
2. Declare its sidecar-derived state, in-process/UI query access, lifecycle,
   default configuration and unsupported interface verbs.
3. Add only the minimal invocation transport and run the shared assertions.
4. If ZotSeek is no longer runnable or inspectable, report that finding and
   propose another in-process Zotero plugin; do not silently substitute one.

## Exit criteria

- [x] pinned ZotSeek revision is runnable and inspectable, or replacement is
      explicitly proposed for the same architecture seat
      — commit `f442f82`, artifact `zotseek-1.21.2.xpi` sha256 `7daaf964`,
      verified at construction. It loads on Zotero 10.0.1 and its source is
      readable; no replacement is needed.
- [x] adapter passes ticket 0578's declaration-only contract checks
      — `tests/test_acceptance_adapter_zotseek.py`, 25 tests, none of which
      needs the host application, the artifact or a display; plus the
      target-neutrality guard, which picks this adapter up from its own `NAMES`
      with no edit anywhere.
- [x] no ZotSeek code is copied into this repository
      — every claim about its behaviour is cited `path:line` into the pinned
      tree or is a measurement. The adapter contains no function, snippet or
      paraphrase of its source.
- [x] every interface verb yields pass, fail, not-offered or not-run
      — read as "no verb produces a fifth thing and no exception escapes": the
      run is 1 pass, 1 fail, 3 not-offered, 0 not-run, and every declared-absent
      verb raises `UnsupportedVerb` (tested). Read literally as "each of the
      seven produces a check state", it is NOT deliverable and that is a
      finding rather than a gap: the layer's five assertions name `install`,
      `query`, `status` and `uninstall`, so `configure`, `pause` and `resume`
      are the subject of no assertion at all and can yield no state. `resume`
      at least appears in the egress drive record as not-offered; `configure`
      and `pause` appear nowhere but the declaration.
- [x] `make check` and `erg check tickets/` pass
